== Meteorological history ==

Hurricane Sahan formed in the mid-[[Ethiopian Ocean|Aethiopean Ocean]] and initially tracked westward toward the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]]. Rather than continuing west across the islands, Sahan turned abruptly northwest, toward [[Lacashe]] and [[Asikyira]]. The sharp change in track from westward to northward took coastal communities by surprise; many preparations had been made for a westward path, and the storm instead came ashore in areas considered off the main hurricane lanes.

== Impact ==

=== Bolodun and Kuvari Islands ===

Sahan destroyed virtually the entire built environment of the island of [[Bolodun]], levelling the capital [[Ominira]] and the island's infrastructure, including [[Ominira International Airport]]. The storm caused the near-total loss of the island's buildings, roads, port, and utilities.

=== Lacashe ===

In [[Lacashe]], Sahan inundated many coastal neighbourhoods of the city of [[Porto Vren]], the free-port border city in the south of the country. The flooding of Porto Vren and the southern coastal districts contributed the majority of the hurricane's fatalities across the region: 12,420 people died in southern Lacashe, many of them flooded to death in their own homes when the storm surge overwhelmed low-lying neighbourhoods. Sahan remained the country's deadliest weather event of the 20th century.

== Aftermath ==

The disaster prompted massive international outreach across the Capuyaquiran region. In the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]], the [[Adonian Community]] led a major reconstruction of the island. During the 1970s, [[Burawa]] invested heavily in hurricane defence infrastructure as part of the rebuilding. The reconstruction rebuilt [[Ominira]] to a raised, hurricane-resistant design: buildings were elevated on stilts or columns, with public life conducted on the second floor and the open ground level used for goods movement in dry weather and cleared for storm surge during heavy rain. The airport was reconstructed on 15 March 1971, with hurricane-resistant construction paid for in part by Burawi investment.

The uneven distribution of aid between the islands in the aftermath of Sahan became a source of grievance in the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]], and was one of the reasons for the secession of [[Narubi]] in 1971. Narubian leaders argued that their province received less reconstruction support than other parts of the union, contributing to the decision to seek independence.

